WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.910
No one has higher hopes than the studio when
it comes to a movie's franchise potential.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:08.120
If they sense a feature could be the start
of something major, they might even green

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.610
light a follow-up before the film even hits
theaters.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:13.969
But in some cases, those sequel plans are
embarrassingly premature.

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:18.440
Here are some movies that were so bad, their
sequels were actually canceled.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.050
The Mortal Instruments: City of Bones

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:25.440
The successes of Twilight and The Hunger Games
made adaptations of young adult novels hot

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.640
in Hollywood.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.550
Sony seemed poised to capitalize by bringing
Cassandra Clare's The Mortal Instruments:

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:34.629
City of Bones to the big screen, with plans
to follow with the second installment, City

00:00:34.629 --> 00:00:35.730
of Ashes.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.670
Unfortunately for the studio, the film was
a disaster with critics and audiences, earning

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.280
back just above half of its production costs.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:47.170
Instead, the series was adapted to the small
screen as the TV series Shadowhunters.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:48.170
Fantastic Four

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:53.440
Fox’s 2015 reboot of Fantastic Four may
have seemed like a good idea at the time,

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.469
especially since it boasted a quartet of critically
favored actors in Michael B. Jordan, Miles

00:00:57.469 --> 00:01:00.000
Teller, Jamie Bell, and Kate Mara.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.250
Critics overwhelmingly hated it, however,
and the box office results were terrible.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:08.080
A sequel was slated for 2017, but it never
went into production.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.560
John Carter

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.170
Disney's John Carter was such a box office
bomb that the film's title is now often used

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:17.719
as an adjective to describe a grossly underperforming
picture.

00:01:17.719 --> 00:01:21.180
The film's critical reception was moderate
at best, but what really destroyed the would-be

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.930
franchise was how much of a loss the studio
took at the box office.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:30.840
The film reportedly cost a whopping $250 million
to make, not counting its marketing expenses,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.590
and earned just $73 million in North America.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:34.630
Ouch.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:35.630
Green Lantern

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:39.850
Although Ryan Reynolds has since proven his
worth in the superhero market by way of Deadpool,

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:44.520
his turn as the Green Lantern was a franchise
nonstarter and remains something of a scar

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:45.520
on his filmography.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.930
Green Lantern tanked at the box office and
Green Lantern 2 lost its greenlight.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.509
Terminator: Genisys

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:57.030
2015's Terminator: Genisys couldn't quite
recapture the fanfare of the original series,

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:01.490
even with the reappearance of Arnold Schwarzenegger
as the titular Terminator as an added boost.

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:05.329
The movie performed poorly with critics and
domestic audiences, which made the cancellation

00:02:05.329 --> 00:02:08.030
of its sequel plans far from surprising.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:10.280
The Cat in the Hat

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.069
Adaptations of Dr. Seuss' books have usually
done well, including How the Grinch Stole

00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:16.870
Christmas, Horton Hears a Who!, and The Lorax.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:21.480
But critics and audiences rejected the 2003
live-action take on one of Seuss' most iconic

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.150
properties, The Cat in the Hat.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.120
The film scored just 10 percent on Rotten
Tomatoes and financially under-performed as

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.120
well.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.680
Seuss' widow Audrey Geisel went so far as
to refuse any further live-action films.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.220
Battlefield Earth

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:38.390
John Travolta's unintentionally hilarious
costume was the least of Battlefield Earth's

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:39.530
problems.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.980
This infamous dud, which was supposed to be
followed up with a sequel to cover the rest

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:47.940
of L. Ron Hubbard's 1,000-page plus sci-fi
epic, was laughed off by reviewers and largely

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.200
ignored by audiences.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.670
Tron Legacy

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:55.790
It took more than two decades for the first
sequel to 1982's Tron to come down the pike,

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.909
and it looks like it might take just as long
for the next one.

00:02:58.909 --> 00:03:04.250
The 2010 movie's domestic box office was mostly
a bust, and despite an animated TV spinoff,

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.060
Disney abandoned plans for another movie.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:07.319
Thanks for watching!

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:10.129
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:10.129 --> 00:03:13.069
And leave us a comment to let us know which
scrapped sequel you’d most love to see some

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:13.129
day.

